  "content": {
    "id": "p19-21d-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Find the implied domain of each of the following functions:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2-2x-8}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,-2]\\cup[4,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-2x-8&=(x-4)(x+2)\\end{align*}$$\nThe upward-opening parabola has zeros at $-2$ and $4$, so it is non-negative outside these zeros, including the endpoints. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=(-\\infty,-2]\\cup[4,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+4x+13}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\mathbb{R}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2+4x+13&=(x+2)^2+9\\end{align*}$$\nThe radicand is at least $9$ for every real $x$. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=\\mathbb{R}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0\\}$",
          "worked": "The denominator cannot be zero, so $x\\ne0$. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0\\}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{3x-6}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[2,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}3x-6&\\ge0\\\\3x&\\ge6\\\\x&\\ge2\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=[2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}+\\sqrt{2-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[-2,2]$",
          "worked": "Both radicands must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}x+2&\\ge0\\\\x&\\ge-2\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}2-x&\\ge0\\\\x&\\le2\\end{align*}$$\nTheir restrictions overlap on $[-2,2]$. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=[-2,2]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-8}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(4,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The square root must be defined and the denominator must be nonzero, so the radicand must be strictly positive.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x-8&>0\\\\2x&>8\\\\x&>4\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=(4,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+2x+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\mathbb{R}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x+4&=(x+1)^2+3\\end{align*}$$\nThe radicand is at least $3$ for every real $x$. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=\\mathbb{R}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+x-6}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,-3]\\cup[2,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+x-6&=(x+3)(x-2)\\end{align*}$$\nThe upward-opening parabola has zeros at $-3$ and $2$, so it is non-negative outside these zeros, including the endpoints. Therefore, $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=(-\\infty,-3]\\cup[2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-21d-q10-i",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "i",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{1-\\sqrt{2-x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p19-21d-q10-i-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[1,2]",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p19-21d-q10-i-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Require both square-root radicands to be non-negative.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The inner square root requires:\n$$\\begin{align*}2-x&\\ge0\\\\x&\\le2\\end{align*}$$\nThe outer square root requires:\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\sqrt{2-x}&\\ge0\\\\\\sqrt{2-x}&\\le1\\\\2-x&\\le1\\\\x&\\ge1\\end{align*}$$\nSquaring is valid because both sides are non-negative. Combining the restrictions gives $\\operatorname{dom}(f)=[1,2]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      }
    ]
  },
